.TH KBDFS 4
.SH NAME
kbdfs \- keyboard file server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kbdfs
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Kbdfs
opens
.B /dev/scancode
(see
.IR kbd (3)),
which diverts the keyboard's raw stream to it -- while it holds the file
open, the kernel's own translation sees nothing -- and serves the
translation back as two files, posted at
.BR /srv/kbdfs .
It takes no arguments.
.PP
.B cons
is the keyboard as a stream of characters, cooked the way
.B /dev/cons
is.
Every reader that wants typed text reads it.
.B kbd
is one message per read, for a reader that wants the keys themselves:
.IP
.EX
c<runes>    the characters typed, as UTF-8
k<runes>    every key held down, after a press
K<runes>    every key held down, after a release
.EE
.PP
A reader of
.B kbd
sees a chord: the alt key held down together with another, which makes no
character and so never appears on
.BR cons .
A window manager reads
.B kbd
for the chords it acts on and passes the rest to whoever has the console.
A read of either file parks until there is something to answer.
.PP
.I Init
(see
.IR init (8))
starts
.I kbdfs
and mounts it at
.B /n/kbd
before starting
.IR intuition (4),
which reads
.B /n/kbd/kbd
for its own chords.
.PP
The last close of
.B /dev/scancode
gives the raw stream back to the kernel's own translation.
The server stops on the pipe's hang-up, when the last mount and the
posted name are both gone.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /srv/kbdfs
the posted service
.TP
.B /cons
the characters typed, as a byte stream
.TP
.B /kbd
one message per read: the keys held, after each change
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/kbdfs
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bind (1),
.IR kbd (2),
.IR ring (2),
.IR ioproc (2),
.IR thread (2),
.IR kbd (3),
.IR intuition (4)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Kbdfs
exits with a number rather than the empty-string convention other
programs use.
Failing to open
.B /dev/scancode
ends it with 116, before it posts anything.
